## What a `type: actor` instance is

A first-class representation of an agent — AI model, human collaborator, organization, or service account — that authors, reviews, or interacts with corpus content.  Actors carry identity-level facts (name, version, capabilities) plus contextual membership (which contexts they run in, what organizations they belong to).  The actor type enables typed-edge provenance — memorials cite their `created-by:` actor; rules apply to specific actors; reviews are attributed to actor instances.

## When an actor entity is warranted

- An AI model version interacts substantively with the corpus (Claude Opus 4.7, GPT-5.4, etc.)
- A human collaborator authors or directs the corpus
- An external service / system contributes substantively (reviewers, broker processes)

Actors are NOT created for transient interactions — a model invoked once for a specific task doesn't need an actor entity.  Actors are for stable identities that recur across sessions.

## Identity-and-context orthogonality

A single actor identity can run in multiple contexts (sandboxed vs unsandboxed; different host environments; different organizational contexts).  Per the actor-context-rules deep-dive ADR, identity is independent of context — runtime context attaches via `runs-in-context:` multi-arity field rather than creating per-context actor instances.

